### Step 7: Database Migration

The migration strategy is mechanical and safe:

For each removed text field on each entity, create a migration that reads the existing string value and inserts a new Note row with the correct TrackType, NoteState of Unset, and the existing string as Content. The old column is then dropped. No content is lost. Every existing text value becomes an Unset note in its corresponding track, correctly representing that it has not yet been reviewed and classified under the new framework.

The NeedsFurtherAnalysis boolean on the existing Note entity migrates as follows: any Note where NeedsFurtherAnalysis is true becomes Flagged. Any Note where it is false becomes Unset. The boolean column is retained temporarily for the migration period then dropped.

Yes. This is the correct architectural decision and it is better than hard labels for several precise reasons.

---

## Why Configuration Beats Hard Labels

Hard labels on an enum mean every track type is a compile-time constant. Adding a new track type requires a code change, a migration, and a recompile. Discovering during data entry that an entity type needs a track you did not anticipate — which will happen — becomes a development task rather than a configuration task.

A configuration-driven approach means the track definitions live in data. Adding a track type for a new entity configuration is a data entry task. The view model does not know or care what the tracks are — it renders whatever the configuration specifies. The question text, the tooltip, the index, the singleton/multi-note constraint, the required/optional status, and the track's cognitive mode label are all properties of the configuration record rather than properties of the enum.

This also means the configuration itself becomes part of your story planner's self-documentation. Opening the configuration for a Character entity shows you the complete epistemological architecture of what a Character entry is supposed to contain, in the same interface where you work with the content. The configuration is the explicit statement of the framework.

---

## The Configuration Record Structure

Each track configuration needs these fields to drive rendering correctly:

```csharp
public class NoteTrackConfiguration
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    // Which entity type this track belongs to
    public EntityType EntityType { get; set; }

    // Display order within the entity's track list
    public int Index { get; set; }

    // Short label shown in the tab or section header
    // e.g. "Psychological Architecture" or "Governing Logic"
    public string TrackName { get; set; }

    // The single question visible during data entry
    // Under 15 words. The operational test for note assignment.
    // e.g. "Who is this character at their core, and why?"
    public string DisplayQuestion { get; set; }

    // Verbose reference text shown in tooltip or expandable panel
    public string Tooltip { get; set; }

    // Whether this track holds one note (singleton) or many
    public bool IsSingleton { get; set; }

    // Whether an empty track is a diagnostic signal
    // Required tracks produce a completion warning when empty
    public bool IsRequired { get; set; }

    // The cognitive mode this track requires
    // Used for visual differentiation and mode-switching cues
    public CognitiveMode CognitiveMode { get; set; }

    // Whether notes in this track carry WorldDate
    public bool SupportsWorldDate { get; set; }

    // Whether notes in this track use NoteState (Unset/Flagged/Confirmed)
    // or the softer Reviewed state used for Development Notes
    public bool UsesSoftConfirmation { get; set; }
}

public enum CognitiveMode
{
    ZeroFocalization,      // Author as historian/physicist
    SceneArchitecture,     // Author as director
    ProjectManagement,     // Author to future self
    Bibliographic          // Author as researcher
}

public enum EntityType
{
    Character,
    CodexWorldPrinciple,
    CodexConstructedIdea,
    CodexTechnology,
    CodexOrganization,
    CodexGeographicReference,
    Chapter,
    StoryThread,
    Theme,
    PlotPoint,
    PlotPointCharacter,
    PlotPointCodexEntry,
    PlotPointStoryThread,
    PlotPointTheme
}
```

---

## What This Enables

The view model queries configurations by EntityType and renders tracks in Index order. Each track section shows TrackName as the header, DisplayQuestion as always-visible helper text, and Tooltip on hover or expand. The CognitiveMode drives a subtle visual distinction — zero focalization tracks might have a neutral color, scene architecture tracks a warmer tone, project management tracks a muted treatment. This visual differentiation reinforces the mode-switching without requiring explicit labels.

Required tracks with no Confirmed notes produce a diagnostic indicator on the entity card in list views. The completion profile the view model computes is entirely driven by configuration — which tracks are required, which are singleton, which need Confirmed notes — without any hardcoded logic about specific track types.

When you discover during data entry that a Geographic Reference entry needs a track you did not anticipate, you add a configuration record. No code change. The next time you open a Geographic Reference entry, the new track renders.

---

## The Unset Queue Integration

The Unset queue becomes a configuration-aware view. Notes in Unset have no track assignment. The queue displays them alongside the configuration for their parent entity, showing all available tracks with their display questions. The assignment gesture is: read the note, scan the display questions, tap the correct one. The question drives the decision. The label is incidental.

This is the practical payoff of the configuration approach — the display question is not documentation about the system. It is the system. The author never needs to remember what goes in Track A versus Track B because the question is always visible at the point of decision.

---

## One Practical Constraint

The NoteTrackConfiguration table needs to be seeded with sensible defaults before first use. The initial seed covers all the tracks defined in this conversation. After that, additions are data operations. The seed script is effectively the compiled version of everything this conversation has established about what belongs where — which means writing it is the final verification that the framework is internally consistent. If any track's display question cannot be written in under fifteen words without losing its specificity, that is a signal the track definition is not yet precise enough.

## Function Keys in WPF

In WPF, most function keys are available for custom binding. The ones with default behaviors to be aware of:

**F1** — by WPF convention opens Help. Not bound at the framework level but many applications and users expect it for help. Worth avoiding or overriding deliberately with awareness.

**F2** — no WPF default. Common convention in Windows for rename operations (Explorer, file dialogs). Available but may feel semantically odd for Historical Record if users have strong muscle memory.

**F3** — no WPF default. Common convention for Find Next in many applications. Available.

**F4** — opens the dropdown on a ComboBox when focused, and Alt+F4 closes the window. F4 alone is available but test carefully that it does not interact unexpectedly with focused controls.

**F5** — no WPF default. Common refresh convention in browsers. Available.

**F6** through **F12** — no WPF defaults. Fully available.

The practical recommendation is to treat F1 as reserved for a help/tooltip toggle that reveals the full tooltip for the currently focused track, rather than binding it to a content track. This respects the convention and gives you a useful feature. Your content track bindings then start at F2, which aligns naturally with a low index number.

---

## The Configuration Extension

```csharp
public class NoteTrackConfiguration
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public EntityType EntityType { get; set; }
    public int Index { get; set; }

    // F1 reserved for help, content tracks start at F2
    // Null means no hotkey assigned for this track
    public int? FunctionKeyNumber { get; set; }

    public string TrackName { get; set; }
    public string DisplayQuestion { get; set; }
    public string Tooltip { get; set; }
    public bool IsSingleton { get; set; }
    public bool IsRequired { get; set; }
    public CognitiveMode CognitiveMode { get; set; }
    public bool SupportsWorldDate { get; set; }
    public bool UsesSoftConfirmation { get; set; }

    // Computed display string shown in the track header
    // e.g. "F2 — Psychological Architecture"
    // Null if no function key assigned
    public string? HotkeyLabel => 
        FunctionKeyNumber.HasValue 
            ? $"F{FunctionKeyNumber} — {TrackName}" 
            : TrackName;
}
```

---

## The WPF Binding

Register the key bindings at the window or user control level so they are active whenever the entity editor is focused, not just when a specific control has focus:

```csharp
// In the entity editor view's constructor or Loaded handler
private void RegisterTrackHotkeys(
    IEnumerable<NoteTrackConfiguration> configs)
{
    foreach (var config in configs
        .Where(c => c.FunctionKeyNumber.HasValue))
    {
        var key = KeyFromFunctionNumber(
            config.FunctionKeyNumber!.Value);

        var binding = new KeyBinding(
            new RelayCommand(() => 
                MoveSelectedNoteToTrack(config.Id)),
            key,
            ModifierKeys.None);

        InputBindings.Add(binding);
    }
}

private static Key KeyFromFunctionNumber(int n) => n switch
{
    1  => Key.F1,
    2  => Key.F2,
    3  => Key.F3,
    4  => Key.F4,
    5  => Key.F5,
    6  => Key.F6,
    7  => Key.F7,
    8  => Key.F8,
    9  => Key.F9,
    10 => Key.F10,
    11 => Key.F11,
    12 => Key.F12,
    _  => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(n))
};
```

The `MoveSelectedNoteToTrack` command on the view model handles the actual assignment and triggers the Unset queue refresh.

---

## The Helper UI Display

Each track section header renders the hotkey label from the computed property. During an active note selection — when a note is focused or selected in the Unset queue — all track headers highlight their function key badge to signal that pressing that key will assign the note. This gives the author a scannable one-glance view of all available assignments without requiring them to remember the mappings.

A small persistent legend strip at the bottom of the editor, visible whenever a note is selected, shows all active function key assignments for the current entity type in a single row. This is the practical equivalent of the tooltip being always-visible at the moment of decision — the author's eye can drop to the strip, read the question associated with each key, and press the correct one without breaking flow.

```
F1 Help  |  F2 Psychology  |  F3 History  |  F4 Macro Arch  |  F5 Development  |  F6 Bibliographic
```

When the entity type changes — switching from a Character to a Codex entry — the legend strip updates to reflect the new entity's configuration. The function key numbers stay consistent across entity types for the same cognitive mode (F2 always means the primary truth track, F3 always means historical record) which allows muscle memory to develop across entity types rather than requiring relearning per entity.

Yes, for the same reason as the track configuration — but the implementation has a meaningful difference worth thinking through before building.

---

## The Case For Configuration-Driven Enums

The UsageType values on PlotPointCodexEntry are the clearest example of the problem. The correct values differ by codex category: a World Principle uses Implied/Observed/Named/Subverted/Demonstrated, a Technology uses Mentioned/ActiveUsage/Definition/Subversion/Failure/FirstReveal, an Organization uses Represented/Acting Collectively/Referenced/Structurally Invoked/Fractured. If these are a single flat enum, every codex connection shows all values regardless of which category the connected entry belongs to. The author has to mentally filter out the irrelevant options every time.

Configuration-driven enum values mean the dropdown for a PlotPointCodexEntry connection shows only the values appropriate for the connected entry's category. The view model queries the configuration using the connected entity's type and renders only the relevant options. Adding a new value for a specific category is a data operation, not a code change.

The same argument applies to GapType on PlotPointCharacter — the values Ironic, Tragic, Closing, Aligned, Paralipsis, Synthesis are not all equally relevant in every scene. Synthesis only applies at the chapter level. Paralipsis requires specific macro architecture conditions. A configuration could suppress irrelevant options based on context.

---

## The Meaningful Difference From Track Configuration

Track configuration maps one-to-many: one configuration record produces one track section in the UI, containing many notes. The relationship is straightforward.

Enum configuration maps to a selection control: one configuration set produces a dropdown or segmented control where exactly one value is selected. The difference matters for the data model.

For tracks, the configuration record references the entity type and the view model renders accordingly. For enums, each configuration record is a valid value for a specific context, and the connection entity stores the selected value's identifier rather than the enum integer.

This means the stored value needs to be stable — if you reconfigure the enum options, existing stored values must remain valid. The cleanest approach is storing the configuration record's Id as the selected value rather than a position-dependent integer. The display label, tooltip, and contextual filtering are all properties of the configuration record. The stored value is just a foreign key.

```csharp
public class EnumValueConfiguration
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    // Which enum this value belongs to
    public EnumType EnumType { get; set; }

    // Which entity type or category this value applies to
    // Null means applies to all contexts
    public EntityType? ApplicableEntityType { get; set; }

    // Display order within the dropdown
    public int Index { get; set; }

    // The label shown in the UI
    public string DisplayLabel { get; set; }

    // Brief explanation shown as tooltip or helper text
    // Answers: when should I select this value?
    public string SelectionGuidance { get; set; }

    // Whether this value triggers additional required fields
    // e.g. FirstReveal triggers Physical Presence Note requirement
    public bool TriggersAdditionalRequirement { get; set; }
}

public enum EnumType
{
    UsageType,
    GapType,
    GoalTrajectory,
    CharacterRole,
    CharacterDevImpact,
    ThemeProminence,
    NoteState,
    DraftStatus,
    TensionPhase,
    CoreDriver,
    ConflictType,
    Presentation
}
```

