## The Practical Boundary

Configure the enums where context-dependent filtering produces meaningfully better UX — UsageType and GapType are the clear cases. Use standard enums for everything else and add tooltips at the UI layer rather than through configuration records.

The configuration table for enums will be small — perhaps thirty to forty records covering UsageType variants across four codex categories and GapType variants across two connection entity types. This is manageable seeding work that produces a meaningful UX improvement at the exact moment of data entry where the author most needs guidance: when assigning a usage type to a connection and wondering which of twelve options applies.

This is the correct extension and it follows directly from the same principle that drove the track configuration decision. If the track types are data rather than code, the connection property types should be data too. The reasoning is identical: hardcoding the property types means every new analytical axis you discover requires a code change and migration. Making the property types themselves configurable means adding a new connection property is a data operation.

