## Remove All Navigation Properties

Yes. Remove them everywhere. The friction you identified — models navigating and view models also navigating, creating two paths to the same data with inconsistent behavior — is the direct consequence of mixing the two paradigms. Navigation properties on models imply that the model layer is responsible for relationship traversal. ICollectionView filtering in view models implies that the view model layer is responsible. Both cannot be true simultaneously without producing the confusion you have been experiencing.

The clean rule is: **model classes are rows, view models are relationships**. A model class knows its own Id and its foreign key Ids. It knows nothing about the objects those foreign keys point to. All relationship traversal — finding which notes belong to this subject, finding which note tracks apply to this plot point, finding the subject definition for a link — happens in the view model layer using filtered views on the in-memory collections.

The complete list of navigation properties to remove:

Subject — remove SubjectDefinition navigation property, keep SubjectDefinitionId int field

PlotPoint — remove Chapter navigation property, keep ChapterId nullable int field

PlotPointSubjectLink — remove NoteTrackDefinitions list, remove NarrativePropertyDefinitions list

Chapter — remove PlotPoints collection

Note — remove all navigation properties (CodexEntry, Character, Theme, Chapter, StoryThread, SourceMaterial), keep all foreign key Id fields

Any remaining navigation property on any model class is a violation of the pattern and should be removed.

