public class NoteTrackViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    private readonly int _ownerId;
    private readonly OwnerType _ownerType;
    private readonly NoteTrackDefinition _definition;
    private readonly IStoryService _storyService;

    public ICollectionView Notes { get; }

    public NoteTrackViewModel(
        NoteTrackDefinition definition,
        int ownerId,
        OwnerType ownerType,
        IViewModelRegistry registry,
        IStoryService storyService)
    {
        _definition = definition;
        _ownerId = ownerId;
        _ownerType = ownerType;
        _storyService = storyService;

        Notes = CollectionViewSource.GetDefaultView(
            storyService.Notes);
        Notes.Filter = FilterNote;
    }

    private bool FilterNote(object obj)
    {
        if (obj is not Note note) return false;
        return note.OwnerId == _ownerId
            && note.OwnerType == _ownerType
            && note.NoteTrackDefinitionId == _definition.Id;
    }

    public string DisplayQuestion => _definition.DisplayQuestion;
    public string TrackName => _definition.TrackName;
    public int? FunctionKeyNumber => _definition.FunctionKeyNumber;
}
```

This is the complete elimination of the dual-navigation friction. The model layer has no awareness of relationships. The view model layer constructs all relationships through filtered views on collections that are already in memory. Every access pattern is consistent and there is only one path to any piece of data.

## Save Immediately on Creation

Save immediately and let EF Core assign the Id. This is the correct approach for your architecture.

When you call `context.Add(entity)` and then `context.SaveChanges()`, EF Core inserts the row and populates the entity's Id property with the database-generated value. SQLite uses autoincrement by default for integer primary keys. The entity in memory gets its Id updated in place — the same object reference you are holding now has a valid Id after the save returns. You can immediately use that Id to associate notes, links, and other related records.

```csharp
public async Task<Subject> CreateSubjectAsync(int subjectDefinitionId, string name)
{
    var subject = new Subject
    {
        SubjectDefinitionId = subjectDefinitionId,
        Name = name
    };

    _context.Subjects.Add(subject);
    await _context.SaveChangesAsync();

    // subject.Id is now populated by the database
    // Safe to add to the in-memory ObservableCollection
    Subjects.Add(subject);

    return subject;
}
```

---

## Why Not Pick the Next Highest Id

Manually assigning Ids by finding the current maximum and adding one is fragile and unnecessary. The problems are specific:

The ObservableCollection and the database can diverge. If your in-memory collection has an entity that was created but not yet saved, its manually assigned Id is provisional. Any code that filters on Id before the save completes is working with an Id that may not match what the database eventually assigns.

Concurrent writes are not an issue for a single-user desktop app, but a logic error in Id assignment is. If you calculate max Id as 47 and assign 48, then a save fails and you retry, you may reassign 48 to a different object.

SQLite's autoincrement is reliable and correct. There is no reason to replicate it in application code.

---

## The Observable Collection Timing

The pattern that works cleanly in your architecture is: create the entity, save immediately, then add to the ObservableCollection. The collection only ever contains entities with valid database Ids. Any view model created from a collection member can immediately use its Id for filtering.

```csharp
// In IStoryService implementation
public async Task CreateSubjectAsync(int subjectDefinitionId, string name)
{
    var subject = new Subject
    {
        SubjectDefinitionId = subjectDefinitionId,
        Name = name
    };

    _context.Subjects.Add(subject);
    await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    // Id is now valid

    Subjects.Add(subject); 
    // UI updates via ObservableCollection notification
    // Any NoteTrackViewModel filtering on this subject's Id 
    // will work correctly immediately
}
```

The alternative — adding to the collection before saving and using a temporary Id — creates a window where the UI can interact with an entity whose Id is not yet valid. In a load-everything architecture where filtering is done by Id, this window produces silent failures: notes filtered by a temporary Id find nothing, or worse, find the wrong notes if the temporary Id happens to collide.

---

## Saving Is Cheap Here

In a load-everything-at-startup SQLite application, individual saves for entity creation are not a performance concern. The cost of a single insert in SQLite is microseconds. The save-on-create pattern is safe, simple, and consistent with your architecture's assumptions. Batch saves and deferred Id assignment add complexity without benefit in this context.
